From 0384f5c660be6541307ee2e360d9567a3affd597 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: timadinorth <timuraiagent@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:02:10 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 27/36] moe: split routed experts by residency across backends

Keep the most used experts of each MoE layer in VRAM and the rest in system
memory, instead of placing whole layers. The router picks few experts per
token, so the same VRAM covers far more of the traffic.

Each expert tensor is split offline into a hot and a cold tensor, each with
n_expert_used trailing zero slots. A foreign assignment at rank iex maps to
slot base + iex, so a token never uses the same slot twice, which the CUDA
MoE path requires. CUDA computes the zero slot and gets zeros; the CPU
backend skips it instead of reading it, which is what keeps the cold branch
cheap.

- ggml_mul_mat_id_set_mask_from marks the mask slots, off by default
- GGML_OP_MOE_BRANCH_IDS maps router ids to one branch, CPU and CUDA
- build_moe_ffn builds two branches and sums them before the weights
- expert_hot_count hparam plus the split tensor names
- scripts/split-moe-experts.py rewrites the GGUF and permutes ffn_gate_inp
- test-backend-ops covers masked MUL_MAT_ID and MOE_BRANCH_IDS

Placement uses the existing -ot mechanism. Dormant on unsplit models.

Measured on Qwen3.8-Flash-Next UD-Q4_K_XL, RTX 5090, 262144 context, q8_0 KV,
at equal VRAM: 20.5 tok/s to 31-36 tok/s on mixed coding work, about 1.6x,
with prefill unchanged and output identical to the unsplit model.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context)
